  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user's request: \"Explain how a relational database executes a SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Write a detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\"\n\nNeed produce final answer. Need detailed technical tutorial. Must use complete sentences? Probably all sentences complete. Avoid repeating. Need cover parsing, planning, indexing, joins, transactions, returning rows, examples, tradeoffs, performance pitfalls. Need probably structure with sections. Need concrete examples SQL and maybe explain optimizer, catalogs, parse, bind, plan, cost, physical operators, execution, index usage, join algorithms, transactions isolation, MVCC, locking, return rows.\n\nNeed ensure no repetition. Could write as tutorial with examples. Need be careful not to repeat phrases? Avoid redundant. We can be thorough.\n\nLet's plan.\n\nTitle: How a relational database executes a SQL query\n\nSections:\n1. High-level pipeline: client sends SQL; server parse, bind/qualify, optimize, execute, return.\n2. Parsing and binding.\n- tokenize, parse to AST, validate syntax.\n- name resolution using catalog: tables, columns, aliases, functions, privileges.\n- bind to internal types, data types, constraints, views expansion, parameter types.\n- example: SELECT c.city FROM customers c WHERE c.country = 'US';\n- parse errors vs semantic errors.\n- tradeoff: strict vs permissive modes; prepared statements reuse parse/plan.\n\n3. Optimization.\n- logical transformation: predicate pushdown, projection pushdown, commutativity, join reordering.\n- cost model: statistics: row counts, distinct values, histograms, correlation, selectivity, page sizes, buffer pool, hardware.\n- choose access paths: sequential scan vs index scan; index scan tradeoffs: narrow filter vs wide rows, index maintenance, coverage.\n- choose join order and join algorithm.\n- cost vs actual: cardinality misestimation; plan caching.\n- example: orders, customers.\nSELECT o.order_id, c.name\nFROM orders o JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id\nWHERE o.status = 'pending';\nOptimizer may push status filter, choose index on status, estimate rows.\n\n4. Execution physical operators.\n- pipeline: operator tree, nested loop, hash join, index nested loop, sort, aggregate, materialize",
